1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 d6 3. Nc3 Nbd7 4. Nf3 e5 5. e4 Be7 6. Be2 c6 7. O-O O-O 8. h3
Re8 9. Qc2 Qc7 10. Be3 Nf8 11. Rad1 Ng6 12. Rfe1 h6 13. Bf1 Nh7 14. d5 Ng5 15.
Nxg5 Bxg5 16. Bxg5 hxg5 17. Qd2 Qb6 18. Qxg5 Qxb2 19. Re3 Nf4 20. Rg3 g6 21.
dxc6 bxc6 22. Rxd6 Qb6 23. Rf3 Qc5 24. Rd8 Qf8 25. Rxe8 Qxe8 26. g3 Nh5 27. c5
Kh7 28. Rd3 Ba6 29. Rd6 Bxf1 30. Kxf1 Rb8 31. Qd2 Ng7 32. Rd7 Ne6 33. Qd6 Rd8
34. Rxd8 Qxd8 35. Qxd8 Nxd8 36. Ke2 Ne6 37. Na4 Ng5 38. Ke3 Nxh3 39. Nb2 Ng5
40. Nc4 f6 41. Na5 Ne6 42. Nxc6 a6 43. f3 Nxc5 44. Kd2 Kg7 45. Kc3 Ne6 46. Nb8
a5 47. Kd3 Kf7 48. Nc6 a4 49. Nb4 g5 50. Nd5 Nd4 51. f4 gxf4 52. gxf4 Ne6 53.
fxe5 fxe5 54. Nc3 a3 55. Kc4 Ke7 56. Nb5 Kd7 57. Kd5 Nf4+ 58. Kxe5 Nd3+ 59. Kf6
Kc6 60. Nc3 Kd6 61. Kf5 Nb4 62. e5+ Ke7 63. e6 Nc6 64. Nb5 Nb4 65. Na7 Ke8 66.
Kf6 Nd5+ 67. Ke5 Nc3 68. Nc8 Kd8 69. Nd6 Ke7 70. Nc8+ Kd8 71. Nd6 Ke7 72. Nf5+
Ke8 73. Kd6 Nb5+ 74. Kc6 Na7+ 75. Kb6 Nc8+ 76. Kc7 Na7 77. Nd4 {"The struggle
between the two Littlewood brothers ex­tended over three days. Sur­prisingly
in view of their reputations, its main interest consisted in its delicately
played end-game. White’s treatment of the opening is well-nigh perfect. 19...
Nf4 concedes a pawn. Black feared 20 Rg3 followed by h4-h5. In the knight and
pawn end­game after 35...Nxd8, Black’s 36...Ne6 and 37...Ng5 recover the
pawn by force. White is able to lure the black a-pawn on to its eventual doom.
The last 20 moves of the game are worth an article in themselves, the win
being balanced on a knife-edge throughout. On move 68 White falters; on move
72 he picks up the thread again. Typical of the Littlewoods’ ideal
temperament for chess: though Norman still thought he had a draw ten minutes
before he resigned, he soon got over his disappointment and it was in the
afternoon after the final session of this game he cheerfully swindled Hindle 
(Rd. 4)." (CHESS)} 1-0

[Event "Hastings Premier 1963/64"]
[Site "Hastings"]
[Date "1963.12.31"]
[Round "2"]
[White "Tal, Mikhail"]
[Black "Gligoric, Svetozar"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "C87"]
[Annotator "Tal, Mikhail"]
[PlyCount "69"]
[EventDate "1963.12.30"]
[EventType "tourn"]
[EventRounds "9"]
[EventCountry "ENG"]

1. e4 e5 {In the Moscow International Tournament of November-December last,
Gligoric adopted the Sicilian Defence against me. In annotating the game, I
wrote that this was a pleasant surprise, as I like playing against the
Sicilian. Perhaps that is why he switched back to the Ruy this time.} 2. Nf3
Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 d6 5. c3 Nf6 6. d4 Bd7 7. Nbd2 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Re1 Re8 {
Reaching the same position as in my game against Gligoric at Zurich, 1959.} 10.
Nf1 ({There I continued} 10. a3 {instead of 10 Nf1 as here, and after,} Bf8 11.
b4 d5 $1 {he won. Here I follow a better method, the same as Matanovic against
Gligoric in the Moscow International Tournament mentioned above.}) 10... h6 {
The best.} (10... Bf8 {would invite} 11. Bg5 h6 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. dxe5 Nxe5 14.
Nxe5 Bxa4 15. Ng4 {with a nice end-game for White.}) 11. Ng3 Bf8 12. h3 ({On} 
12. Bc2 {at once would come} Bg4 {: Black’s QB on that square can be quite a
nuisance in the Ruy Lopez.}) 12... Na5 13. Bc2 ({This is better than} 13. Bxd7
{as played by Unzicker in a well-known game against Schmid, mentioned in
Barden’s book The Ruy Lopez.}) 13... c5 14. d5 {I was content to block the
centre, thus simplifying the defence for my opponent to some extent, because I
felt I had enough on the king’s side to decide the game.} b5 15. b3 {To
prevent ...Nc4.} Nb7 {Black is, for a long time, virtually a piece down, as
his knight on b7 is almost useless.} 16. Be3 {Planning Qd2, Nh2 and f4; the
type of plan which is always good against Black’s “Benoni” central pawn
formation if only he cannot then play . . . e5xf4 and occupy e5 with a bishop
or knight. Here, he cannot readily do that, so the plan is a good one.} g6 {
This and his next form an extremely good counter-plan.} 17. Qd2 h5 $1 18. Rf1 (
{For now if I play} 18. Nh2 {Black will continue} h4 $1 19. Ngf1 Nh5 $1 {
He has taken the initiative on the king’s side where it would now be
difficult for me to make any progress.}) 18... Bg7 ({I feel that} 18... Be7 {
would have been a little better, hindering my plan.}) 19. Bg5 {Gaining a tempo,
to free my knight to go to h2.} Qc7 20. Nh4 (20. Nh2 {would have been more
precise. In playing 20 Nh4, I allowed myself to be tempted by an interesting
combination— see my note to 22...Qd8.}) 20... Nh7 21. f4 exf4 ({Not} 21...
Nxg5 22. fxg5 {; the pawn on g5 would be far too strong.}) ({nor} 21... f6 22.
Nxg6 $1 fxg5 23. fxg5 Qd8 24. h4 Bg4 25. Bd1 {; the -h5-pawn falls and White
should have an easy win. White can cheerfully “sacrifice” when he is
already a piece up (Black’s N on b7)!}) 22. Bxf4 Qd8 $1 {This gains Black a
valuable tempo.} ({If} 22... b4 23. e5 dxe5 24. Bh6 bxc3 25. Rxf7 $3 Kxf7 ({
now if} 25... cxd2 {White mates in 2}) ({if} 25... Bxh6 26. Qxh6 {is even more
drastic}) 26. Bxg6+ {soon wins.}) 23. Nf3 ({I was attracted here by the idea of
} 23. Nhf5 $5 gxf5 24. Nxh5 {, e.g.} Bf6 $3 {This sets up an effective defence,
with ...Rg8 to follow.} (24... Be5 25. exf5 f6 26. Rf3 {White’s attack may
well be worth the little material sacrificed; always bearing in mind that
Black’s knight on b7 is a dead duck}) (24... fxe4 25. Nxg7 Kxg7 26. Bh6+ {
with a fine attack})) 23... b4 {Black must counter-attack energetically,
otherwise there will come 24 Rae1 and e5 cramping him severely.} 24. cxb4 Bb5
25. Rfe1 {There is no attraction in giving up a rook for Black’s b5 B,
whereas White is quite willing to waste a move to give up a rook for the other
bishop, thus denuding Black’s king’s side where he has to watch the black
squares carefully now.} Bxa1 26. Rxa1 h4 ({Not} 26... cxb4 {or 26...Qf6} 27. e5
$1) 27. Nh1 ({If} 27. Ne2 {then} Bxe2 28. Qxe2 Qf6 {wins a piece.}) ({If} 27.
Nf1 Bxf1 28. Rxf1 cxb4 29. Qxb4 Nc5 {would leave me with not enough for the
exchange. Now, however, the knight can make for g4—a good attacking square.})
27... cxb4 28. Nf2 ({If} 28. e5 dxe5 29. Bxe5 {Black has} Ng5 $1 {, e.g.} 30.
Nxh4 ({or it} 30. Nxg5 Rxe5 {threatening ...Rxd5 and ...Exg5 as well as ...Re2.
}) 30... f6 {and, if the attack­ed bishop moves, 31. . .Re2!}) 28... Qf6 29.
Re1 Rac8 {Threatening ...Qc3, at least forcing a draw by repetition, e.g. 30
Bb1 Qc3 31 Qd1 Qf6.} 30. e5 ({Any other day except New Year’s eve, I might
perhaps have, rather pig-headedly, prevented Black’s next by} 30. Be3) 30...
Rxc2 $3 (30... dxe5 $2 31. Bxe5 {followed by Ng4 would, of course, have
yielded White a fine attack.}) 31. exf6 ({Because} 31. Qxc2 {would allow} Qxf4
{, queens and rooks come off as well and the position simplifies out to
complete equality.}) 31... Rxd2 32. Rxe8+ Bxe8 33. Bxd2 Nxf6 34. Bxb4 Nxd5 35.
Bd2 {Source: CHESS, January 1964, Vol.29 no.451, p135-137} 1/2-1/2

